Integer types named by what they are, used everywhere in ca instead of the C names:

| Alias | Type |
|---|---|
| `i8`, `i16`, `i32`, `i64` | signed, exactly that many bits |
| `u8`, `u16`, `u32`, `u64` | unsigned, exactly that many bits |
| `usz` | the type of `sizeof`: sizes, counts, indices |
| `isz` | the type of a pointer difference |
| `sint`, `uint` | the native `int` and `unsigned int`, where an API wants exactly those |
| `byte` | `unsigned char`: raw memory, may alias any object |

The exact-width aliases are selected by bit width (`ca::sint_of<B>`, `ca::uint_of<B>`) among
the standard integer types instead of being assumed, so they are right on every data model and
fail to compile on a target lacking one. `ca::bits<T>` counts the bits of any type.

```c++
#include "ca/integers.hpp"

static_assert(sizeof(ca::u16) == 2U && sizeof(ca::i64) == 8U);
static_assert(ca::bits<ca::u32> == 32U);
static_assert(ca::bits<ca::usz> == ca::bits<void*>);

template <unsigned B>
[[nodiscard]] constexpr auto all_ones(void) noexcept -> ca::uint_of<B> {
	return static_cast<ca::uint_of<B>>(~ca::uint_of<B>{0U});
}

static_assert(all_ones<8U>() == 0xFFU);
static_assert(all_ones<64U>() == 0xFFFF'FFFF'FFFF'FFFFU);

auto main(void) -> int {
	const int values[] = {3, 1, 4, 1, 5};
	const ca::usz count = sizeof(values) / sizeof(values[0]);
	const ca::isz distance = &values[4] - &values[0];
	const ca::byte* const raw = reinterpret_cast<const ca::byte*>(&values[1]);
	unsigned sum = 0U;
	for (ca::usz i = 0U; i < sizeof(int); ++i)
		sum += raw[i];
	return count == 5U && distance == 4 && sum == 1U ? 0 : 1;
}
```

Literals take a suffix when unsigned (`0U`, `1U`), and sizes are `usz` even where an `int`
would fit, so arithmetic on them never mixes signedness.
